Z||000001||Nashco||08-17-2001||12:22 AM||||I, as well, would like to hear about rollers for the 88 V6.  I've seen lots on different rocker ratios, but not much on roller cam and lifter setups...anybody know more about this?  If it's not a direct bolt-in, what would be required to the best of your knowledge?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leadfoot||08-17-2001||12:27 AM||||Welcome, I see you are trying to get some more power from the 2.8 by reducing component friction.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">  I don't think the roller lifters are commercially available since some type of stablizing/anti-rotating system (proper tech term?) would have to be made.  Do a search in the tech section and see what you come up with cause I could be wrong.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shaun41178||08-17-2001||01:05 AM||||you can't use a roller cam with the Fiero intake type setup.  The lifters will hit the underside of the intake manifold.  You will need to use a Gen 3 intake system to get to be able to use a roller cam as the Gen 3 V6 is the first one to use a roller cam.<P>I have already looked into this as I wanted to do it as well but to no avail.<P><P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||08-17-2001||02:04 AM||||You CAN'T use a roller cam setup? *gets mad scientist look, cackles, and rubs hands together*  I'll have to take a look closer at what you mean next time I'm into my motor <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||00lE||08-17-2001||10:15 AM||||You might be able to retrofit the roller cam setup from a 3100/3400 onto a 2.8 block?  The Fiero V6 intake would work just fine, with only a slight modifcation required:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/calgaryfieros/3400engine.htm</A> <P>I think it would be better to just obtain a low mileage 3100/3400 shortblock, and go from there.  This is actually quite an inexpensive alternative, for example, here is a VERY recent auction on eBay that would probably make the expense of rebuilding an older 2.8 look like a joke:<BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=594051363" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=594051363</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DreXteR||08-17-2001||10:46 AM||||Actually, Crane makes roller lifters for 173ci. But arent cheap, also the roller rockers- expensive too. As far as the cam go GM makes 1, but dont know ywt if its work-* For my understand It should work*-.<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000007||Nashco||08-17-2001||11:59 AM||||I knew it couldn't have been too involved.  Looks like it was actually pretty easy, for the performance and durability gains made.<P>Thanks for the link 00|E...is that your engine?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||24.253.26.60||reg||3
Z||000008||Joe Torma||08-17-2001||12:14 PM||||Its not involved if you start with a 3.1, 3.4 FWD block like they did.  Don't bother using a 2.8/RWD block.  You need to machine way too much in the lifter area.<P>FYI the camshaft his hollow... less weight, spins up faster!<P>Joe<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3400_1.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||00lE||08-18-2001||07:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>I knew it couldn't have been too involved.  Looks like it was actually pretty easy, for the performance and durability gains made.<P>Thanks for the link 00|E...is that your engine?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>     There may be a bit of misunderstanding here.. While the intake is not an obstacle as I pointed out with the 3400 conversion link (not mine btw..), retrofiting a roller cam on a non-roller block design would NOT be an easy accomplishment.<P>     Its all a matter of how to handle the anti-rotation device needed for the lifters on a roller cam setup.  The 3100/3400's use a plate that bolts to the block to accomplish this. While (as I noted), it may be possible to mount this plate on a 2.8 block, it would still present a quite a challenge to even an experienced machinist.  <BR>     A better alternative may be roller lifters with built in anti-rotation bars.  Either method would require some machine work be done to the block, which is why I suggest just using a 3100/3400 block, rather than trying to modify a non-roller block.  <BR>     These newer blocks also offer additional reinforcement, powder metal caps, and a much better oil pan design (cast aluminum w/built in windage tray).<P>     The roller cam that GM offers is nothing more than the same "stock" roller cam that was used in all 3100 and 3400 motors.<BR>     It seems that GM actually improved the 60 degree motor design throughtout the years, which might explain the subsequent increases in horsepower. :-)<P>It is important to note that <B>ALL</B> stock connecting rods used in GM 60 degree V6 engines were <B>"FORGED"</B> units capable of withstanding up to 7000 rpm, so modifying small block rods to fit the V6 is (IMO) a waste of money for all but the most extreme  racing applications (eg. not street performance rebuilds).<P>||216.160.241.203||reg||1
Z||000010||ironhedno3||08-18-2001||12:02 PM||||well put ooie...||||reg||6
Z||000011||skitime||08-18-2001||11:14 PM||||Jimmy Welcome to the forum.  I told you that you would get answers here.  Hope to see you on the forum often.  Thanks for allowing us to visit and see you great cars.||||reg||1
Z||000012||DreXteR||08-18-2001||11:56 PM||||00|E - Why is so dif to use a roller cam into a non roller block?<P>Thanx<P>DreX ||208.234.57.212||reg||4
Z||000013||00lE||08-19-2001||09:22 AM||||    <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DreXteR:<BR><B>00|E - Why is so dif to use a roller cam into a non roller block?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Unlike a normal hydraulic lifter, the lifters used with a roller cam must not be allowed to rotate.  On a block not designed for a roller cam, some provision for an anti-rotation device must be made. This can be either a guide plate or a special roller lifter system that incorporates a bar mounted across two adjacent lifters:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/roller_lifter.jpg"> <BR>  In this case, the block must be machined to allow clearance of these anti-rotation bars.  The 3100/3400 uses a guide plate system as can be seen (at the base of the pushrods) in this picture:<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/3100internal.jpg">   <P>While it may not be impossible, I am sure you can imagine just how difficult the mounting of these guide plates could be on a block not designed to accept them.  You can also see some of the addtional 3100/3400 block reinforcement (the w-shaped webing) in the above pic.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 00lE (edited 08-19-2001).]||216.160.241.244||reg||1
Z||000014||Shaun41178||08-19-2001||10:02 AM||||ok I guess I was wrong.  I was actually told that by Phil Huff at Focoa about the roller cam.  He said the lifters would hit the lower fiero intake.  This was back before the Gen 3 came out so Phil might have tried using an older style anti-rotation lifter setup like the bars and thats what would hit the intake.<P>But yes I guess starting with a 3400 block would be the easiest way to go.<P>I was looking at my Aluminum block and it doesn't look like it has those provisions to add the anti-rotation system to it as a bolt on.  Looks like the block would have to be machined.  Would be kind of cool to have an all aluminum all roller engine in the back of a Fiero wouldn't it?<P>------------------<BR>Shaun 85 GT with nitrous<BR>0-60 in 5.6 seconds||||reg||1
