Z||000023||fierosound||08-22-2001||10:50 PM||||You cannot use regular lifters with roller cams.  True, the roller lifters have less 'drag' on the powertrain. But, the cam lobes are more aggressive with steeper ramps for opening and closing the valves.  This can be done BECAUSE a roller lifter ROLLS on the lobe and the contact patch of lobe/roller is very small.  A flat bottomed hydraulic lifter needs a more gradual ramp on the cam lobe, and could end up gouging the steeper lobes of a roller cam. (yes, wer're talking thousands of an inch)<P>I think you could use roller lifters on a regular cam, but what would be the point??  You'd just have less lifter drag, but but not gain anything from the cam over using regular hydraulic lifters for it.   The roller cams lobe profiles are made POSSIBLE because of the roller lifters.  It is this COMBINATION that gives you increased power.<P>For more info see here -  <A HREF="http://www.amotion.com/tech/camtalk.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.amotion.com/tech/camtalk.html</A> <BR>||||reg||10
Z||000024||88formula||08-22-2001||11:27 PM||||You can’t use roller lifters on a tappet camshaft because the lodes on a tappet camshaft are tapered. The taper helps spin a flat tappet lifter so that the lifter and cam lobe don’t just rub against each other. If you look at a flat tappet camshaft closely you will see that the bottoms are actually convex too.<P>To my knowledge there are no retrofit roller camshafts for a block that was designed for a flat tappet camshaft.     <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000025||Joe Torma||08-22-2001||11:31 PM||||Only use roller lifters with roller cams. The cams are heat treated differently and will not wear correctly if you mix them.  Regular flat tappet cams are not flat, so you can't use a roller lifter.  When I was researching my 3400 cam I found out there are mystery roller cam cores for the 2.8 out there(my cam source had em), but nobody knows what to do with them.<P>Also FYI, the roller lifters used in the 3400 are stubby compared to the Duke/V8 lifters.  The lifter bore centerlines are much closer than the V8/Duke, so you can't even use that style anti-rotation pieces.  You may be able to fabricate your own, if you can harden metals properly.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||DreXteR||08-23-2001||04:49 PM||||OK, lets get this straigth! Lot of similar info but pointing in dif directions. <P>Facts: Roller lifters with roller cam, nothing else. Hydraulic cam with hydraulic lifters.<P>Crane make roller lifters for 173ci, they are mechanical and use vertical locking bar. Now we have aftermarket roller lifters. Also I talk to some shops and the SBC use the same diameter lifters that uses the 173. That means, maybe the SBC rollers work on the 173. Now, the issue is the tall they are. How much, I dont know yet- but will. <P>My plan in my rebuld - Knowing the GM cam WORK into 173 non-roller, also knowing the SBC lifters FIT(dont know if works) and knowing the SBC roller rockers WORK too (must be narrow body) --- Guess!!! ---  Thats rigth!!! CHEAP PERFORMANCE PARTS!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>I will get GM stock roller cam + SBC roller rockers + SBC roller liftrs (if works). This setup will be cheaper than the roller rockers for 173.<P>Joe Torma - Define "stubby"<P>DreX<p>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 08-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||DreXteR||08-26-2001||11:51 PM||||Top||||reg||1
Z||000028||Joe Torma||08-27-2001||01:47 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DreXteR:<BR><B>Crane make roller lifters for 173ci, they are mechanical and use vertical locking bar. Now we have aftermarket roller lifters. Also I talk to some shops and the SBC use the same diameter lifters that uses the 173. That means, maybe the SBC rollers work on the 173. Now, the issue is the tall they are. How much, I dont know yet- but will. <P>My plan in my rebuld - Knowing the GM cam WORK into 173 non-roller, also knowing the SBC lifters FIT(dont know if works) and knowing the SBC roller rockers WORK too (must be narrow body) --- Guess!!! ---  Thats rigth!!! CHEAP PERFORMANCE PARTS!!     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>I will get GM stock roller cam + SBC roller rockers + SBC roller liftrs (if works). This setup will be cheaper than the roller rockers for 173.<P>Joe Torma - Define "stubby"<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry, must have missed the post.<P>Stubby is about 3/8"  I guess.  So, it won't work.  The oil hole won't be in the right spot....and where the lifter rides on the bore is wrong too, plus the bore centrlines on the 173 are much closer than a SBC.  Yes they are the same dia.<P>So you've got:<BR>Aftermarket Roller Rockers(Crane Gold Narrow body)+Stock GM roller Cam+Crane Mechanical lifters.<P>What are you looking for performance wise?  The stock GM roller cam isn't rowdy.<P>Intersting note, the GEN III heads on my 3400 have pedistal mounted roller fulcrum rockers.<P>Joe<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||DreXteR||08-27-2001||10:46 AM||||Joe - Now I understand the lifters issue, sb dont work   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   For roller rockers-they do. I talk to jegs and the roller lifters for 173 are $248.99   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> Ouch! But is the only way to go. I still looking to get sb roller rockers, the one that fits are for lt1, must be narrow body, non-self aligned, 3/8 stud. <P>Also about the GM roller cam, I will use forced induction. As far my reseach goes the bests cams usually are the stock ones. Also Im want to use 1.6 roller rocker for an extra lift. I choose GM roller cam just for 2 things, 1.Cheaper 2.Stock specs.  If I can get an aftermarket roller cam for almost the same prize of the GM, sure will go for the aftermarket.<P>THanx<P>DreX<BR><p>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 08-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Joe Torma||08-27-2001||11:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DreXteR:<BR><B>Also Im want to use 1.6 roller rocker for an extra lift. I choose GM roller cam just for 2 things, 1.Cheaper 2.Stock specs.  If I can get an aftermarket roller cam for almost the same prize of the GM, sure will go for the aftermarket.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>There are no cores for the roller cam.  The lobes are pressed onto a hollow tube then a ball is shoved through it to secure them.  You'll have to have a stock cam "worked on" like I did.  Stick with the stock profile, you'll be good to go.<BR>||||reg||1
